+# Firebase Setup for Cattle Manager%0D%0A%0D%0AThis document explains how to set up Firebase for the Cattle Manager app to enable authentication and multi-user sync functionality.%0D%0A%0D%0A## Prerequisites%0D%0A%0D%0A- Android Studio%0D%0A- Google account%0D%0A- Firebase project%0D%0A%0D%0A## Step 1: Create Firebase Project%0D%0A%0D%0A1. Go to %5BFirebase Console%5D(https://console.firebase.google.com/)%0D%0A2. Click %22Create a project%22 or %22Add project%22%0D%0A3. Enter project name (e.g., %22cattle-manager%22)%0D%0A4. Enable Google Analytics (optional)%0D%0A5. Click %22Create project%22%0D%0A%0D%0A## Step 2: Add Android App to Firebase%0D%0A%0D%0A1. In your Firebase project, click %22Add app%22 and select Android%0D%0A2. Enter your package name: %60com.jumblemint.cows%60%0D%0A3. Enter app nickname: %22Cattle Manager%22%0D%0A4. Enter SHA-1 certificate fingerprint (see below for how to get it)%0D%0A5. Click %22Register app%22%0D%0A%0D%0A### Getting SHA-1 Certificate Fingerprint%0D%0A%0D%0ARun this command in your project root:%0D%0A%60%60%60bash%0D%0A./gradlew signingReport%0D%0A%60%60%60%0D%0A%0D%0AOr in Android Studio:%0D%0A1. Open Gradle panel (right side)%0D%0A2. Navigate to: app %3E Tasks %3E android %3E signingReport%0D%0A3. Double-click to run%0D%0A4. Copy the SHA1 fingerprint from the output%0D%0A%0D%0A## Step 3: Download Configuration File%0D%0A%0D%0A1. Download the %60google-services.json%60 file%0D%0A2. Replace the placeholder file at %60app/google-services.json%60 with the downloaded file%0D%0A%0D%0A## Step 4: Enable Authentication%0D%0A%0D%0A1. In Firebase Console, go to %22Authentication%22%0D%0A2. Click %22Get started%22%0D%0A3. Go to %22Sign-in method%22 tab%0D%0A4. Enable %22Google%22 sign-in provider%0D%0A5. Add your app's SHA-1 certificate fingerprint if not already added%0D%0A6. Save the configuration%0D%0A%0D%0A## Step 5: Set Up Firestore Database%0D%0A%0D%0A1. In Firebase Console, go to %22Firestore Database%22%0D%0A2. Click %22Create database%22%0D%0A3. Choose %22Start in test mode%22 (for development)%0D%0A4. Select a location for your database%0D%0A5. Click %22Done%22%0D%0A%0D%0A### Firestore Security Rules (Development)%0D%0A%0D%0AFor development, you can use these permissive rules:%0D%0A%0D%0A%60%60%60javascript%0D%0Arules_version = '2';%0D%0Aservice cloud.firestore %7B%0D%0A match /databases/%7Bdatabase%7D/documents %7B%0D%0A // Allow authenticated users to read/write their own data%0D%0A match /herds/%7BherdId%7D %7B%0D%0A allow read, write: if request.auth != null;%0D%0A %0D%0A match /cows/%7BcowId%7D %7B%0D%0A allow read, write: if request.auth != null;%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A %0D%0A match /activities/%7BactivityId%7D %7B%0D%0A allow read, write: if request.auth != null;%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A %0D%0A match /pastures/%7BpastureId%7D %7B%0D%0A allow read, write: if request.auth != null;%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A %0D%0A match /notes/%7BnoteId%7D %7B%0D%0A allow read, write: if request.auth != null;%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A %0D%0A match /users/%7BuserId%7D %7B%0D%0A allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId;%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A%7D%0D%0A%60%60%60%0D%0A%0D%0A### Production Security Rules%0D%0A%0D%0AFor production, implement proper security rules:%0D%0A%0D%0A%60%60%60javascript%0D%0Arules_version = '2';%0D%0Aservice cloud.firestore %7B%0D%0A match /databases/%7Bdatabase%7D/documents %7B%0D%0A // Users can only access their own user document%0D%0A match /users/%7BuserId%7D %7B%0D%0A allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId;%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A %0D%0A // Herd access control%0D%0A match /herds/%7BherdId%7D %7B%0D%0A // Check if user is a member of this herd%0D%0A function isHerdMember() %7B%0D%0A return exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/herds/$(herdId)/members/$(request.auth.uid));%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A %0D%0A allow read: if request.auth != null && isHerdMember();%0D%0A allow write: if request.auth != null && isHerdMember();%0D%0A %0D%0A // Herd members subcollection%0D%0A match /members/%7BuserId%7D %7B%0D%0A allow read: if request.auth != null && isHerdMember();%0D%0A allow write: if request.auth != null && %0D%0A (resource.data.role == 'OWNER' && request.auth.uid == resource.data.userId);%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A %0D%0A // Herd data subcollections%0D%0A match /%7Bcollection%7D/%7BdocId%7D %7B%0D%0A allow read, write: if request.auth != null && isHerdMember();%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A %7D%0D%0A%7D%0D%0A%60%60%60%0D%0A%0D%0A## Step 6: Update String Resources%0D%0A%0D%0A1. Open %60app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml%60%0D%0A2. Replace %60YOUR_WEB_CLIENT_ID_HERE%60 with your actual Web Client ID from Firebase%0D%0A3. You can find the Web Client ID in your %60google-services.json%60 file or in Firebase Console %3E Project Settings %3E General tab%0D%0A%0D%0A## Step 7: Test the Setup%0D%0A%0D%0A1. Build and run the app%0D%0A2. Try to sign in with Google%0D%0A3. Check Firebase Console %3E Authentication to see if users are being created%0D%0A4. Check Firestore to see if data is being synced%0D%0A%0D%0A## Architecture Overview%0D%0A%0D%0AThe app uses a layered architecture:%0D%0A%0D%0A- **Room Database**: Local storage and offline support%0D%0A- **Firestore**: Cloud storage and real-time sync%0D%0A- **Repository Pattern**: Abstracts data sources%0D%0A- **Sync Service**: Handles bidirectional sync between Room and Firestore%0D%0A%0D%0A### Data Flow%0D%0A%0D%0A1. User actions modify local Room database immediately%0D%0A2. Sync service uploads changes to Firestore%0D%0A3. Real-time listeners detect remote changes%0D%0A4. Remote changes are merged into local database%0D%0A5. UI updates automatically via Flow/LiveData%0D%0A%0D%0A### Multi-User Support%0D%0A%0D%0A- Users authenticate with Google%0D%0A- Each user can create or join herds%0D%0A- Herds contain cattle, activities, pastures, and notes%0D%0A- Real-time sync keeps all herd members in sync%0D%0A- Offline support allows work without internet%0D%0A%0D%0A## Troubleshooting%0D%0A%0D%0A### Common Issues%0D%0A%0D%0A1. **SHA-1 Certificate Mismatch**: Make sure you've added the correct SHA-1 fingerprint%0D%0A2. **Package Name Mismatch**: Ensure Firebase project uses %60com.jumblemint.cows%60%0D%0A3. **google-services.json**: Make sure the file is in the correct location (%60app/google-services.json%60)%0D%0A4. **Internet Permission**: Ensure %60INTERNET%60 permission is in AndroidManifest.xml%0D%0A5. **Google Play Services**: Make sure Google Play Services is installed on test device%0D%0A%0D%0A### Debug Steps%0D%0A%0D%0A1. Check Android Studio logs for Firebase initialization errors%0D%0A2. Verify %60google-services.json%60 is properly configured%0D%0A3. Test authentication in Firebase Console%0D%0A4. Check Firestore rules and data structure%0D%0A5. Verify network connectivity%0D%0A%0D%0A## Security Considerations%0D%0A%0D%0A1. **Never commit** %60google-services.json%60 to public repositories%0D%0A2. Use proper Firestore security rules in production%0D%0A3. Implement proper user input validation%0D%0A4. Consider data encryption for sensitive information%0D%0A5. Regularly review Firebase security settings%0D%0A%0D%0A## Next Steps%0D%0A%0D%0A1. Implement proper error handling%0D%0A2. Add offline conflict resolution%0D%0A3. Implement user invitation system%0D%0A4. Add data export/import functionality%0D%0A5. Set up Firebase Analytics for usage tracking
