-# Firebase Setup for Cattle Manager
+# Firebase Google Sign-In Setup Instructions

-This document explains how to set up Firebase for the Cattle Manager app to enable authentication and multi-user sync functionality.
+The Google Sign-In feature is currently not working because Firebase is not properly configured. Here's how to fix it:

-## Prerequisites
+## Current Issue
+- The `strings.xml` file contains a placeholder: `YOUR_WEB_CLIENT_ID_HERE`
+- The `google-services.json` file has an empty `oauth_client` array
+- This prevents Google Sign-In from working properly

-- Android Studio
-- Google account
-- Firebase project
+## Steps to Fix

-## Step 1: Create Firebase Project
+### 1. Firebase Console Setup
+1. Go to [Firebase Console](https://console.firebase.google.com/)
+2. Select your project: `herdmanager-8eeb7`
+3. Go to **Authentication** → **Sign-in method**
+4. Enable **Google** as a sign-in provider
+5. Add your app's SHA-1 fingerprint (see step 2)

-1. Go to [Firebase Console](https://console.firebase.google.com/)
-2. Click "Create a project" or "Add project"
-3. Enter project name (e.g., "cattle-manager")
-4. Enable Google Analytics (optional)
-5. Click "Create project"
-
-## Step 2: Add Android App to Firebase
-
-1. In your Firebase project, click "Add app" and select Android
-2. Enter your package name: `com.jumblemint.cows`
-3. Enter app nickname: "Cattle Manager"
-4. Enter SHA-1 certificate fingerprint (see below for how to get it)
-5. Click "Register app"
-
-### Getting SHA-1 Certificate Fingerprint
-
-Run this command in your project root:
+### 2. Get SHA-1 Fingerprint
+Run this command in your project directory:
 ```bash
 ./gradlew signingReport
 ```
+Copy the SHA-1 fingerprint from the debug keystore.

-Or in Android Studio:
-1. Open Gradle panel (right side)
-2. Navigate to: app > Tasks > android > signingReport
-3. Double-click to run
-4. Copy the SHA1 fingerprint from the output
+### 3. Update Firebase Project
+1. In Firebase Console, go to **Project Settings**
+2. Under **Your apps**, find your Android app
+3. Click the gear icon → **Add fingerprint**
+4. Paste your SHA-1 fingerprint
+5. Download the updated `google-services.json` file

-## Step 3: Download Configuration File
-
-1. Download the `google-services.json` file
-2. Replace the placeholder file at `app/google-services.json` with the downloaded file
-
-## Step 4: Enable Authentication
-
-1. In Firebase Console, go to "Authentication"
-2. Click "Get started"
-3. Go to "Sign-in method" tab
-4. Enable "Google" sign-in provider
-5. Add your app's SHA-1 certificate fingerprint if not already added
-6. Save the configuration
-
-## Step 5: Set Up Firestore Database
-
-1. In Firebase Console, go to "Firestore Database"
-2. Click "Create database"
-3. Choose "Start in test mode" (for development)
-4. Select a location for your database
-5. Click "Done"
-
-### Firestore Security Rules (Development)
-
-For development, you can use these permissive rules:
-
-```javascript
-rules_version = '2';
-service cloud.firestore {
- match /databases/{database}/documents {
- // Allow authenticated users to read/write their own data
- match /herds/{herdId} {
- allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
- 
- match /cows/{cowId} {
- allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
- }
- 
- match /activities/{activityId} {
- allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
- }
- 
- match /pastures/{pastureId} {
- allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
- }
- 
- match /notes/{noteId} {
- allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
- }
+### 4. Replace Files
+1. Replace `app/google-services.json` with the new file
+2. The new file should have OAuth client entries like:
+```json
+"oauth_client": [
+ {
+ "client_id": "your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com",
+ "client_type": 1,
+ "android_info": {
+ "package_name": "com.jumblemint.cows",
+ "certificate_hash": "your-sha1-hash"
 }
- 
- match /users/{userId} {
- allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId;
- }
+ },
+ {
+ "client_id": "your-web-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com",
+ "client_type": 3
 }
-}
+]
 ```

-### Production Security Rules
+### 5. Update strings.xml
+The `default_web_client_id` in `strings.xml` should be automatically updated by the Google Services plugin, but if not, replace `YOUR_WEB_CLIENT_ID_HERE` with the web client ID from the JSON file.

-For production, implement proper security rules:
+## Current Demo Mode
+Until Firebase is properly configured, the app shows:
+- A warning about Firebase configuration
+- A demo button that explains what would happen
+- Debug information showing the current state

-```javascript
-rules_version = '2';
-service cloud.firestore {
- match /databases/{database}/documents {
- // Users can only access their own user document
- match /users/{userId} {
- allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId;
- }
- 
- // Herd access control
- match /herds/{herdId} {
- // Check if user is a member of this herd
- function isHerdMember() {
- return exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/herds/$(herdId)/members/$(request.auth.uid));
- }
- 
- allow read: if request.auth != null && isHerdMember();
- allow write: if request.auth != null && isHerdMember();
- 
- // Herd members subcollection
- match /members/{userId} {
- allow read: if request.auth != null && isHerdMember();
- allow write: if request.auth != null && 
- (resource.data.role == 'OWNER' && request.auth.uid == resource.data.userId);
- }
- 
- // Herd data subcollections
- match /{collection}/{docId} {
- allow read, write: if request.auth != null && isHerdMember();
- }
- }
- }
-}
-```
+## Testing
+Once configured:
+1. The sign-in button will work normally
+2. Users can sign in with their Google accounts
+3. Data will sync across devices (when sync backend is implemented)
+4. The debug card can be removed from the SignInScreen

-## Step 6: Update String Resources
-
-1. Open `app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml`
-2. Replace `YOUR_WEB_CLIENT_ID_HERE` with your actual Web Client ID from Firebase
-3. You can find the Web Client ID in your `google-services.json` file or in Firebase Console > Project Settings > General tab
-
-## Step 7: Test the Setup
-
-1. Build and run the app
-2. Try to sign in with Google
-3. Check Firebase Console > Authentication to see if users are being created
-4. Check Firestore to see if data is being synced
-
-## Architecture Overview
-
-The app uses a layered architecture:
-
-- **Room Database**: Local storage and offline support
-- **Firestore**: Cloud storage and real-time sync
-- **Repository Pattern**: Abstracts data sources
-- **Sync Service**: Handles bidirectional sync between Room and Firestore
-
-### Data Flow
-
-1. User actions modify local Room database immediately
-2. Sync service uploads changes to Firestore
-3. Real-time listeners detect remote changes
-4. Remote changes are merged into local database
-5. UI updates automatically via Flow/LiveData
-
-### Multi-User Support
-
-- Users authenticate with Google
-- Each user can create or join herds
-- Herds contain cattle, activities, pastures, and notes
-- Real-time sync keeps all herd members in sync
-- Offline support allows work without internet
-
-## Troubleshooting
-
-### Common Issues
-
-1. **SHA-1 Certificate Mismatch**: Make sure you've added the correct SHA-1 fingerprint
-2. **Package Name Mismatch**: Ensure Firebase project uses `com.jumblemint.cows`
-3. **google-services.json**: Make sure the file is in the correct location (`app/google-services.json`)
-4. **Internet Permission**: Ensure `INTERNET` permission is in AndroidManifest.xml
-5. **Google Play Services**: Make sure Google Play Services is installed on test device
-
-### Debug Steps
-
-1. Check Android Studio logs for Firebase initialization errors
-2. Verify `google-services.json` is properly configured
-3. Test authentication in Firebase Console
-4. Check Firestore rules and data structure
-5. Verify network connectivity
-
-## Security Considerations
-
-1. **Never commit** `google-services.json` to public repositories
-2. Use proper Firestore security rules in production
-3. Implement proper user input validation
-4. Consider data encryption for sensitive information
-5. Regularly review Firebase security settings
-
-## Next Steps
-
-1. Implement proper error handling
-2. Add offline conflict resolution
-3. Implement user invitation system
-4. Add data export/import functionality
-5. Set up Firebase Analytics for usage tracking
+## Note
+The app works perfectly in local-only mode without Firebase configuration. Users can use all features with local storage.","refToolCallId":"toolu_01JckKPZ9757uZ1t9dpepmpf"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me also add a demo mode that simulates a successful sign-in for testing purposes. Let me create a demo user creation function:"},{"type":"toolCall","toolCallId":"toolu_01WCRFUZLw8hfeFUHfBY1RfK","statusText":"Change generated","status":"SUCCESS","context":{"files":[{"path":"app/src/main/java/com/jumblemint/cows/auth/AuthService.kt","fsPath":"C:/Users/jeepi/AndroidStudioProjects/cows/app/src/main/java/com/jumblemint/cows/auth/AuthService.kt"}]},"isHidden":false},{"type":"codeHunk","lineBasedDiff":"--- C:/Users/jeepi/AndroidStudioProjects/cows/app/src/main/java/com/jumblemint/cows/auth/AuthService.kt
